    "body": "General Motors will prepare its Lansing Delta Township Assembly plant in Michigan to build hybrid vehicles, the company announced. The plant currently builds three gas-powered, three-row SUVs: the Buick Enclave, Chevrolet Traverse and GMC Acadia.\nWhat’s Related\nThe hybrid plan is part of a bigger commitment to the area. GM said its investments in the Lansing region will top $2 billion over the next several years. That total includes $1.25 billion GM announced earlier for its Lansing Grand River Assembly plant.\nGM hasn’t said which hybrids the plant will build or when production will start. It said model names and timing will come closer to launch. Reuters, citing unnamed sources, reported that the factory will produce hybrid versions of the Traverse, Enclave and Acadia. GM hasn’t confirmed that.\nFederal money moves to hybrids\nTo help pay for the work, GM and the U.S. Department of Energy are restructuring a federal grant previously awarded to GM. The money, from DOE’s Domestic Manufacturing Conversion Grant program, will now go toward hybrid production at Lansing Delta Township.\nTechCrunch reported that the grant was originally $500 million. The Biden administration awarded it to convert a different Lansing factory to build electric vehicles . GM told the outlet the money was never paid out.\nRELATED: Who Owns Which Car Brands? A Comprehensive Guide\nA record year in Lansing\nThe plant turns 20 this year. It built more than 291,000 vehicles in 2025, its highest yearly total. GM didn’t say whether the hybrid work will add jobs .\n“Lansing’s strength starts with its people. Their skill and experience are why we continue to invest here,” said Mike Trevorrow, GM’s senior vice president of Global Manufacturing. “We’re building on what this team does well today while preparing for what customers will need in the years ahead.”\nHe added, “That means investing in our people, our facilities, and the ability to build a broad range of vehicles.”\nGM to spend more on U.S. manufacturing\nGM expects to spend about $9 billion on U.S. manufacturing and more than $7 billion on U.S. research and development this year. It has also confirmed investments at Orion Assembly in Michigan, Spring Hill Assembly in Tennessee, and Fairfax Assembly in Kansas. GM says those projects will push its U.S. production above 2 million vehicles a year once they’re fully running.",
